From: plaz@netcom.com (Geoff Dale)
Subject: Meta: Re: Thoughts on "PPLs"

Dave Krieger said:
[Excellent discussion of the use and state of alt.extropians deleted,
because I agree and back it 100%.]
>It is my hope that, at that time, the list can stop
>trying to be all things to all people and go back to the "Enlightened
>Despot" system, which worked fine when I was running the list.

I wasn't on the list at the time and so unfortunately can't comment
knowledgably. My impression, however, is that the list has evolved into a
distinctly different animal than it was in those days.
 
>I disagree with the majority of the ExI board on the usefulness of allowing
>multiple systems of privately produced law (vulgarly, "PPLs") within the
>framework of a single list.  Since Harry and ExI actually keep the list
>running, it is necessarily Harry's baby.  "He who has the tools makes the
>rules," as tcmay said.  Any so-called "PPLs" established are simply Harry's
>lap-dogs, frankly, and their success or failure proves nothing.  The hue
>and cry over "PPLs", the rules for starting one, the penalties for trying
>to get out of them, and so forth, contain no signal as far as I am
>concerned.

I doubt that I could accurately be described as Harry's "lap dog". I do
take your point about the PPLs powers stemming from Harry and the board.

My creation of the AnarchyPPL has done, for me and my subscribers, what
myself and others have tried unsuccessfully to do several times before. It
got rid of several ridiculous rules (no setting the record straight, public
plonkings, etc). On top of that, it rigidly defines the basics. It has also
created a mutual protection society, an advocate against the vagaries of
the List Management.
 
>Replace "PPL" with "State" wherever it occurs, and you see how little we
>have succeeded in getting our heads outside the statist paradigm.  Harvey
>Newstrom has said, "I don't want to belong to *any* PPL" and has been told
>"You *have* to belong to one.  If you don't like any of the existing ones,
>you have to start your own."  Doesn't this sound just like a crop of
>governments condemning "permanent tourists" and stateless persons?  If I
>object to governments in principle, must I start one of my own just to
>avoid being governed by others?  Are "jailer" and "inmate" the only two
>roles available?

However much I may agree with the last paragraph, it seems to argue against
your central point. Are you saying it's unstatist to erect an "enlightened
despot" as opposed to forcing someone to be in a PPL?

>At least one "PPL" has been proposed that goes back to what the original
>list, shorn of the current outbreak of legalistic complexity, was supposed
>to be, which is SQUASH ANYTHING THAT ISN'T SIGNAL:  flames, meta
>discussions, "me toos", and squabbles over the basics.  Countermeasures for
>non-signal posts were once quick, simple, effective, and above all, QUIET
>(through PRIVATE e-mail).  Consistency wasn't a problem because "list
>justice" erred on the side of caution:  anything remotely close to being
>non-signal raised an exception, and usually a word to the wise was
>sufficient.

Which PPL was this? I must of missed it.

>Now when I download my mail, I find twenty percent of the messages are
>META: bullshit, thirty percent are cries for a re-examination of the
>"merits" of deathism, statism, or theocracy;

30%, jeez! Either you and I are reading a different list, or I've really
had my ::excludes set properly. I rarely get anything of this time.

>and forty percent are
>ad-hominem tripe ("X is a Nazi sociopath!" "Y is an escargot-swilling,
>Derrida-spouting relativist Frog!" "Z is emotionally unstable!")  The
>remaining ten percent is as high in quality as ever, but is fast becoming
>not-worth-it.  (And the size of the ::exclude database is, in its present
>state, woefully insufficient to substitute for active policing of
>non-signal posts.  A limit of forty or so ::excludes doesn't begin to
>screen out enough loony individuals and threads to be remotely useful,
>especially since the prefix requirement is honored mostly in the breach
>rather than the observance.)

I'd say forty ::excludes is pretty durn near the same as ::exclude all, if
you pick 'em right. You'd get an occasional message from a newbie. There
just aren't that many regular contributers to discussion here. I know
you've told me you've tried this, but I'd still be amazed if it can't be
done.

Likewise, an ::exclude all + forty ::includes would give you a fairly high
signal list. You'd miss some signal, but that'll happen in an "enlighten
despotism" as well.

I very rarely use ::exclude thread myself. I agree that isn't overly
effective. The couple times I have used it, tho I've been very thankful
that it's been there.

>What's my prescription?  If you want to take a crack at managing S/N for a
>mailing list, you should, if you have any balls, start one of your own,
>running at a different site and without any dependency on Harry, Panix, or
>the current list software.

I'm not looking at just the S/N, there are other factors at work here.
Besides, I'm not the type to leave something just because I'm not 100%
satisfied with it. Improvement is still possible and unlike you, I'd have
to pronounce that this list "doesn't suck". I'm *fairly* happy with it.

One list member's signal, is another list member's noise.

>A state-owned Macy's across the street from a
>state-owned Gimbel's is *not* a "market system".  Start your own list, and
>run it any way you want:  moderate it; edit everything that goes out over
>it; make it a one-way channel for you and you alone to send whatever you
>feel like to your subscribers; or make it a virtual free-for-all where
>flaming, spoofing, out-right lying, and advocacy of mandatory euthanasia
>are permitted.  But put Spontaneous Order to the test through variation
>(real variation, not separate departments within a single J.C. Penney
>store) and selection.

You can do spontaneous order & PPL inside a single mailing list, the same
way you can do "evolution" in Genetic Programming. You have to set up the
"physics" of the universe in such a way as to promote this to happen. Ray's
suggestions are excellent in this regard. I'm mulling over my own additions
and should be posting them soon.

I'm tired of the "its useless, it can't be done, its only playing house"
brand of negativism here. What ever happened to Dynamic Optimism? Or the
Intelligent use of technology? Let's not quit before we start to look at
the problem! Sheesh. If Spontaneous Order won't work here in the den of
those who want it to work, where will it work?

>When the noise gets bad enough on this list, I will start one of my own,
>probably on netcom, and invite people to subscribe.  I will run it my way
>(as described above) and I will have delusions of being neither an
>experimental crucible "testing whether PPL can work" nor a democracy.  And
>I predict that I will have no shortage of either subscribers or signal.

In spite of our differences of opinion here I wish you the best of luck in
all your endevours. You know I mean this.

