Date: Fri, 31 Dec 1993 20:09:31 -0800
From: plaz@netcom.com (Geoff Dale)
Subject: PPLs without power

>   The current PPLs I see being proposed are nothing more than "playing
>house" They have no real power and there is little consequence to NOT
>joining one (being an outlaw has no consequence)

This obviously calls for the removal of the rule that states you must be in
a PPL. I didn't like this at first, but then I decided I do for reasons
I'll discuss later. (Outlaw, btw, has too many conotations I suggest
free-lancers or Nick was using the term "armidillo".)

The "playing house" negative comment I'll happily forgive. It downplays the
benefit already received, a change in the list rules for those who didn't
like it. Since Ray backs this up with constructive criticism, I'm happy as
a clam.

>   Here is my proposal to remedy this situation:
>
>1) PPL owners will be able to remove the posting ability of their clients

1a) PPL owners will be able to remove the mail receiving ability of their
clients. Since the real value in this list is the information flow, I think
this will be as effective as kicking someone off the list. Kind of a
temporary ban.

>2) PPL owners will have a "super exclude" which overrides any of their
>client's settings. (e.g. they can ban Pandit Singh from being seen
>by _any_ of their clients. Sorta like kicking him off the list. also,
>one PPL can exclude another PPL meaning any posters from a rogue PPL
>will not be seen by yours)

This and the ability to "::exclude PPL <foo>" would act as a market force
to kill very noisy PPLs. If you are in a PPL that is so noisy that almost
everyone has it excluded there is a distinct motivation to get out, if you
ever want to be heard.

Certain subgroups may effectively end up in their own list.

>3) if you are an outlaw (not a member of a PPL), then you will be
>subject to a "1 warning rule"  Simply put, if you are not a member of
>a PPL (no protection), then any PPL on the list can "hunt you down" and
>"shoot you" That is, any PPL owner can remove your posting
>ability but they can not reinstate it (because you're dead)
>
>   If you have been shot, your only recourse is to join a PPL and be
>resurrected (the PPL owner can turn on your posting ability if you're
>a client)

It first I thought this was a silly game-like affectation, but then I
realized it's needed to balance out the free-lancers. This "models" the
reason for Law Enforcemnt and Mutual Protectoin societies quite well.

It has interesting applications for applying a similar system to MOOs.

>  Inter-PPL threats could take the form of releasing "criminals"
>(previously banned posters) on the other PPL.

Or ::excluding the whole ppl.

>4) when the list goes to a "for pay" system, part of the subscription
>free will go into your list account as list currency which is tradable
>like thornes. Only if your list credits go to zero will you be required
>to renew your subscription

I was considering this as well, but it works more like taxation and
subsidies or contracts than a free market system. Without a good billing
system, what can you do? (I am looking into joining an existing project for
doing internet billing, called Internet Mercantile Protocol, but I haven't
heard much out of them lately.)

>(posting/receiving mail deducts credits
>as does list services like AGREPing the database and resending)

Was this a suggestion or a planned feature? I've heard nothing about this.

>5) to prevent outlaws from simply forming PPLs themselves to prevent
>being shot, a minimal membership requirement will need to be met before
>the anti-shooting protection kicks in.

I like this idea. In the real world a small mutual protection society would
be pretty ineffective. The question is how small is small? The biggest
independant PPL hasn't quite hit a size of ten yet. The bigger it gets the
harder it will be to start a new one.

>  If these proposals were enacted, we could eliminate Harry's fiat
>regulation and have PPL's spontaneously self regulate because of market
>conditions and internalized costs. PPL's would have real "guns" and
>powr instead of relying on members to shut their mouths or begging
>Harry to remove them from the list.

I very much approve of removing the central regulatory powers. (see Dave
Kreiger's lap dog comment in his message 'Meta: alt.extropians?, plus
thoughts on "PPLs"'  #93-12-1338.

A couple of further suggestions:

A) The changing from one PPL to another be automated. (::subscribe PPL
<foo>). Otherwise, Harry is going to go nuts. I would like the PPLs to
alleviate Harry of stress, not add to it.

B) The default PPL be brain damaged. I suggest that PPL-1 be put into a
recieve-only mode. That way newbies would be, by default, quiet. The
welcome message should obviously mention PPLs and we'd have to provide a
way to get the charters on each PPL. In addition no functional PPL gets an
unfair advantage of being the default.

C) The Nightly PPL Market Report - a cron job submitted every 3:00am or so
generates a report about what PPLs are in existance and their populations.
In addition the top X number of excluded posters and PPLs are listed,
ranked by the number of individuals who aren't recieving them. (It would be
way cool if the job could be done from the user's perspective. What percent
of the population has excluded me? And my PPL? But I suspect this might be
too intensive, since the job would have to be run once per user.)

Well thats enough for me, on New Years eve.

Once more into the future dear friends! May 1994 trip your trigger!

