Date: Sat, 1 Jan 94 02:37:33 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Ecosystem Genome Project (was Khmer Vert - Seal Rant)

Ray Cromwell talks about the possibility of saving the DNA of every nearly
extinct species:

> [...]

  Without regard to whether you worry about the living creatures or not, I
think this is a good idea, and just about feasible with present technology.
If you were serious about reconstructing species at some future date, you
would probably want samples from enough individuals to get a reasonable
amount of genetic variation -- I have heard that 100 individuals is a
lot in this context.  (If we could sequence the DNA we could just use
diff files, since almost certainly much of it would be the same in each
species.)
  A rather more interesting aspect of such a program would be getting enough
of the animals' culture recorded to get the reconstructed species going.
By "culture" I refer to information passed from critter to critter, including
from generation to generation, outside the genome.  Many creatures do this,
as witness the difficulties in habituating animals reared in captivity to
the wild; they lack what they would have learned under the guiding paw of
one or more of their parents.
                                         -- Jay Freeman

PS:  The business about animal culture is whimsically interesting in the
     context of occasional comments here about "when do we get beyond mere
     biological evolution?"  One can make a reasonable (but not conclusive)
     case that information-passing outside the genome is perhaps more than
     two hundred million years old, in which case, we are on our way...

