Date: Sat, 1 Jan 1994 13:25:15 -0500 (EST)
From: Arthur Hlavaty <hlavaty@panix.com>
Subject: 12-Step Groups: Threat or Menace?

	I suppose I could start with "My name is Arthur and I'm an
addict."  I say that out loud almost every day.
	There isn't much to say about it in this context.  I always knew
that the secret of successful drug abuse was "Don't get greedy," but
eventually I got greedy, to the point where I can't use them at all, and
so I have joined a group (narcotics Anonymous) which helps me refrain from
using.  I haven't had any drugs in over a year now.
	The reason I'm telling you all this is that some people,
unfamiliar with actual 12-step groups seem to think in terms of a large
amorphous blob, including 12-step, recovery from abuse, and a scattering
of newage, paganism, and political correctness.  (Rather like Collier and
Horowitz's map of the sixties counterculture which was simultaneously a
bunch of doped-out self-indulgent hippies and a rigorously disciplined
movement carrying out the ideas of International Communism.)  It
occurs to me that some of you might find interest in a more detailed map.
	One distinction which seems important is between groups that focus
on past traumas and groups like NA and AA, which focus on present and
continuing behavior change.  The former sort of group may degenerate into
"I'm a hepless Victim.  You can't expect me to do anything right" and "my
childhood was more abusive than yours," but I wouldn't know; I haven't
spent much time in such groups.  (My own childhood offered evidence for
the universality of Philip Larkin's "They fuck you up, your mum and dad,"
but was mercifully free of the horrors others have endured, such as
beating, rape, being told that it would fall off if played with, etc.)
	In any event, NA has a different approach, concentrating on
getting and staying off drugs now, one day at a time.  (incidentally, I am
not terribly familiar with the groups for alcohol, gambling, etc., but I
suspect that this map fits them a little more loosely than it fits NA.) 
To be sure, NA does attribute our past sins to the "disease" of addiction
(reminding us that we may still be held accountable for them), but
emphasizes that when we put down the drugs, we become responsible for all
our actions.
	People do not dwell upon their past horrors at NA meetings.  Such
are briefly mentioned by the speakers (NA meetings in this area are
divided, roughly in half, into an opening statement (often
autobiographical), and a discussion led by the speaker), but not
emphasized.  Present and future conduct is the focus. 
	Another little-known fact about NA is that it is very anarchistic
and decentralized.  There are no experts or professionals, no higher
degrees of membership.  Individual groups are largely autonomous.  There
are regional and national boards, but they largely deal with things like
trademark protection.  The organization is strictly limited; there are no
"NA communes."  In one of my favorite anarchist books, _De-Managing
America_, Richard Cornuelle mentions 12-step groups as an example of the
kind of good, voluntary organization that will spring up the State's claws
can be pried loose from our lives.
	I don't consider 12-step groups a panacea.  They seems to work for
those who have a specific behavior they want to give up, and are at least
willing to hear talk about a "Higher Power."
	So here I am.  I haven't asked anyone here for hugs or warm
fuzzies, and I don't intend to start now.  But despite my social failings
(I was a high-school nerd and I'M PROUD OF IT), I have some sense of
appropriate conversation in a particular setting.

